d with anise or fennel seed, cumin, cinnamon, turmeric, salt and pepper. It is mainly prepared for Novruz in Azerbaijan along with pakhlava and shekerbura."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-shorgoghal"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shorgoghal"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Azerbaijani cuisine","cuisine/azerbaijani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Azerbaijani cuisine","links":[["Badambura","dish/badambura"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Doghramaj","dish/doghramaj"],["Dovga","dish/dovga"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Shekerbura","dish/shekerbura"],["Sheki halva","dish/sheki-halva"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/51/%C5%9Eorqo%C4%9Fal%C4%B1.JPG/500px-%C5%9Eorqo%C4%9Fal%C4%B1.JPG","w":500,"h":422,"by":"Urek Meniashvili","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9Eorqo%C4%9Fal%C4%B1.JPG"}},"ingredient/curry-paste":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/curry-paste","n":"Curry paste","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Curry paste is a mixture of ingredients in the consistency of a paste used in the preparation of a curry. There are different varieties of curry paste depending on the region and also within the same cuisine. It is generally a mixture of fine-powdered spices with either oil and water or coconut milk; the fat content helps extract the flavor. Some cuisines (e.g. Thai) favor the use of a curry paste over a curry powder."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-curry-paste"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry_paste"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Thai curry","dish/thai-curry"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/80/Patak%27s_sauces_for_sale_in_Asda.jpg/500px-Patak%27s_sauces_for_sale_in_Asda.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Victuallers","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patak's_sauces_for_sale_in_Asda.jpg"}},"dish/vieta-dish":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/vieta-dish","n":"Vieta (dish)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chechen cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Vieta is a Chechen national dish of flax seeds. A thick liquid paste-like mass obtained from roasted or simply dried flax seeds, ground in a mill. It has long been used by Chechens as a nutritional tonic, as well as in the treatment of various diseases. Vieta has long been common among Chechens. An exquisite dish was prepared from ground flax seeds — flax halva — by frying flax flour in boiling oil with sugar or honey. This dish was especially appreciated by the Chechens. Chechens cook the dish only with flax seeds. Before the Caucasian War, Chechens, in addition to dishes, also made linen from flax."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-vieta-dish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vieta_(dish)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chechen cuisine","cuisine/chechen-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Chechen cuisine","links":[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Khingalsh","dish/khingalsh"],["Zhizhig-galnash","dish/zhizhig-galnash"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/44/%D0%92%D0%B8%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0.jpg/500px-%D0%92%D0%B8%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0.jpg","w":500,"h":632,"by":"Takhirgeran Umar","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%B8%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0.jpg"}}}